---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

const title = "Why I Use “HanCHWang” Everywhere | Han Wang";
const description =
  "The personal story behind Han Wang's online ID, hanchwang, and the CH carried forward from his birth name, Han-Cheng Wang.";
const articleJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  headline: "Why I Use “HanCHWang” Everywhere",
  description,
  datePublished: "2026-08-12",
  dateModified: "2026-08-12",
  inLanguage: "en",
  author: {
    "@type": "Person",
    name: "Han Wang",
    url: "https://hanchwang.github.io/",
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: "https://hanchwang.github.io/blog/why-hanchwang/",
};
---

<MainLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/blog/why-hanchwang/"
  type="article"
  jsonLd={articleJsonLd}
>
  <a class="blog-back" href="/blog/"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Blog</a>

  <article class="blog-article">
    <header class="blog-article-header">
      <p class="blog-meta">Personal note · <time datetime="2026-08-12">August 12, 2026</time></p>
      <h1>Why I Use “HanCHWang” Everywhere</h1>
      <p class="blog-article-lede">
        The short answer is that those two letters carry a small piece of my own history.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="blog-article-body">
      <p>
        You may have noticed that I use <strong>hanchwang</strong> in many places online, including
        the address of this website: <a href="https://hanchwang.github.io/">hanchwang.github.io</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The “CH” is not a reference to Switzerland. It is easy to read it as the Swiss country code,
        especially now that I live and work in Zürich, but that is not where it came from. It comes
        from <em>Cheng</em>, the romanization of the Chinese character <span class="inline-hanzi" lang="zh-Hans">成</span>.
      </p>

      <p>
        When I was born, my name was <span class="inline-hanzi" lang="zh-Hans">王汉成</span>, written in English as
        <strong>Han-Cheng Wang</strong>. Later, my name was changed to <span class="inline-hanzi" lang="zh-Hans">王汉</span>, or
        <strong>Han Wang</strong>.
      </p>

      <aside class="name-history" aria-label="The history of my name">
        <div class="name-history-row">
          <span class="hanzi" lang="zh-Hans">王汉成</span>
          <span class="name-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span class="romanization">Han-Cheng Wang</span>
        </div>
        <div class="name-history-row">
          <span class="hanzi" lang="zh-Hans">王汉</span>
          <span class="name-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span class="romanization">Han Wang</span>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <p>
        The character <span class="inline-hanzi" lang="zh-Hans">成</span> no longer appears in my current name, but the
        “CH” stayed with me online. It became a quiet link between the name I was given at birth and
        the name I use today.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is why I often use <strong>HanCH Wang</strong>—or simply <strong>hanchwang</strong>—as my ID across websites,
        accounts, and projects. It is not a country code or a random set of letters. It is a small
        personal trace that I have carried forward.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</MainLayout>
